    "narrative": "Today\u2019s optimal lineup for LawDog hinges on balancing opportunistic matchups against elite arms. Our strongest play is **Tyler Freeman** (60.09), who benefits from a favorable park environment that offsets the threat posed by Freddy Peralta. While he isn't a power-first option, the combination of his park adjustment and a high-efficiency stolen base opportunity against Peralta makes him a core contributor for today\u2019s 5x5 targets. Similarly, **Cal Raleigh** (74.56) stands as our clear anchor; facing Martin P\u00e9rez (4.73 xERA) at the #2 spot provides him the volume and platoon advantage necessary to capitalize on his elite raw power. **Gavin Sheets** (59.95) also grades out strongly, as his ability to handle right-handed pitching, combined with a favorable SP matchup, allows him to provide better relative value than several of our infield options today.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the lineup was navigating the bottom of our batting order against top-tier pitching. **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (55.68) remains a locked-in start, though his score is notably depressed by a matchup with Cole Ragans. The tightest call on the bench was **Gleyber Torres** (46.63); despite a slight boost from wind conditions and an assumed #2 slot, his inability to eclipse the utility production of **Matt Chapman** (47.05) or the speed-adjusted upside of **Jeremiah Jackson** (47.33) kept him on the pine. We are monitoring **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Lawrence Butler** closely, as both rely on \"Lineup Pending\" assumptions\u2014specifically, **Perdomo** is staring down a high-risk matchup against Paul Skenes, where his value is entirely contingent on maintaining that lead-off spot to maximize plate appearances. Given the high-end talent like **Yainer Diaz** and **Bryson Stott** remaining unavailable or benched, we must rely on our efficient speed profiles from **Masyn Winn** and **Jeremiah Jackson** to keep us competitive in the SB category while surviving these challenging pitching rotations.",
